{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block title %}Learn · Market Risk Dashboard{% endblock %}
{% block content %}
<article class="prose">
  <p class="eyebrow">Learn</p>
  <h1>How this dashboard works</h1>
  <p>The dashboard watches {{ r.confidence.total }} indicators that have historically moved US stocks and bonds,
    scores each one, and combines them into one overall risk score. Every number can be traced back to its data.</p>

  <h2>1. Scoring one indicator</h2>
  <p>Each indicator is compared with <strong>its own history</strong>. If today's credit spread is wider than on 90% of
    past days, its risk score is 90. For indicators where a <em>low</em> reading is the danger (like the yield curve),
    the score is flipped so that a high score always means more risk.</p>
  <p>A few indicators have well-known danger lines that matter more than the percentile, such as an inverted yield curve
    or the Sahm Rule. When one of these is crossed, the score is raised to at least red, and the card says why.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><span class="chip chip-green"><span class="icon icon-green" aria-hidden="true">●</span> Green</span> score below {{ AMBER }}: normal.</li>
    <li><span class="chip chip-amber"><span class="icon icon-amber" aria-hidden="true">▲</span> Amber</span> {{ AMBER }} to {{ RED }}: worth watching.</li>
    <li><span class="chip chip-red"><span class="icon icon-red" aria-hidden="true">■</span> Red</span> above {{ RED }}: riskier than almost all of history.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Combining them</h2>
  <p>Indicators are grouped into 9 categories. A category's score is the average of its indicators. The overall score
    is the average of the categories, each weighted equally, so a category with 8 indicators doesn't outvote one with 2.
    The overall score needs at least 5 categories with data.</p>
  <ul>
    {% for floor, name in BANDS|reverse %}<li><strong>{{ name }}</strong>: {{ floor }}{{ "+" if loop.last else "–" ~ (BANDS|reverse|list)[loop.index][0] }}</li>{% endfor %}
  </ul>
  <h3>Why the bands are 40 / 60 / 75</h3>
  <p>They started as round numbers. The evidence for them is the table on <a href="why.html">Why this score?</a>, which
    shows what happened after past days in each band. If the bands work, big stock drops should become more common from
    Calm to High. If they stop doing that, the bands should change, and the table will show it.</p>

  <h2>3. No peeking at the future</h2>
  <p>A past day's score uses <strong>only data that had been published by that day</strong>. Inflation for March, for example,
    only counts from its release in mid-April. Without this, history would look far more predictable than it really was.
    An automated test checks it: adding newer data must never change an older day's score.</p>

  <h2>4. Revised data</h2>
  <p>Some government statistics (jobs, inflation, GDP) are revised months or years later. FRED gives today's revised
    numbers, not what people saw at the time. Indicators like this carry a <span class="badge">Revised data</span> badge;
    treat their past readings as approximate. Market prices (yields, spreads, the VIX) are never revised.</p>

  <h2>5. The economic regime</h2>
  <p>Separately from the risk score, the dashboard classifies the economy by two directions: is <strong>growth</strong>
    speeding up or slowing (Chicago Fed activity index, jobless claims, building permits), and is <strong>inflation</strong>
    speeding up or slowing (core PCE over 6 months versus 12 months)? That gives four regimes. To avoid flip-flopping,
    a new regime must be signalled for about 2 months in a row before the dashboard switches to it. Stocks and bonds
    have tended to behave differently in each regime, as shown on the overview.</p>

  <h2>6. Known limitations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>History is short in places.</strong> Some series start in the 2000s, so their scores compare against fewer crises.</li>
    <li><strong>Few crises to learn from.</strong> There have only been a dozen or so major sell-offs in the data, so any statistic about them is uncertain.</li>
    <li><strong>Hindsight in the design.</strong> The indicators were chosen knowing what happened in 2008, 2020, and other crises.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing indicators.</strong> Some useful measures have no free data source; they're listed under "Not tracked yet" on the overview.</li>
    <li><strong>It describes conditions, not timing.</strong> A high score has often come months before a decline, or with no decline at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-moves-markets">What moves markets</h2>
  <p>Scheduled events and news topics that regularly move US stocks and bonds. They appear on the overview's
    "Coming up" and "In the news" panels as context; none of them change the risk score directly.</p>
  <h3>Scheduled events</h3>
  {% for t in cal_types.values() %}
  <details class="term"><summary>{{ t.name }}</summary>
    <p class="small">{{ t.why_it_matters }}</p>
    <p class="small"><strong>Watch:</strong> {{ t.what_to_watch }} <strong>Usual reaction:</strong> {{ t.typical_reaction }}</p>
  </details>
  {% endfor %}
  <h3>News topics</h3>
  {% for t in topics.values() %}
  <details class="term"><summary>{{ t.name }}</summary><p class="small">{{ t.why_it_matters }}</p></details>
  {% endfor %}

  <h2 id="presidential-cycle">The presidential cycle</h2>
  <p>Some investors believe stocks follow the 4-year presidential term: weaker in the midterm year, stronger in the
    year before an election. Here is what the data shows since 1950 (calendar-year returns, median):</p>
  {% if cycle %}
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year of the term</th><th>Years</th><th>S&amp;P 500 (median)</th><th>Years stocks fell</th><th>Bonds (median)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{% for c in cycle %}<tr {% if c.current %}class="row-now"{% endif %}><th scope="row">{{ c.name }}{% if c.current %} <span class="now-tag">Now</span>{% endif %}</th>
      <td>{{ c.years }}</td><td>{{ c.stocks_median|pct(true) }}</td><td>{{ c.stocks_down_years }} of {{ c.years }}</td><td>{{ c.bonds_median|pct(true) }} <span class="range">({{ c.bond_years }} years)</span></td></tr>{% endfor %}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  {% endif %}
  <p><strong>Treat this pattern with real caution.</strong> Each row has only about 19 years, a single year can swing the
    median, and the pattern has failed often (stocks rose strongly in some midterm years and fell in some pre-election years).
    It is not a reason to buy or sell. What does matter around elections is uncertainty about policy, which the dashboard
    tracks through its policy-uncertainty indicators. Bond returns before late 2003 are estimated.</p>

  <h2 id="glossary">Glossary</h2>
  <dl class="glossary">
    {% for g in glossary.values()|sort(attribute="term") %}
    <dt id="term-{{ g.id }}">{{ g.term }}</dt>
    <dd>{{ g.definition }}</dd>
    {% endfor %}
  </dl>

  <h2>Disclaimer</h2>
  <p>This dashboard is for learning and monitoring. It is not financial advice and does not recommend buying or selling anything.</p>
</article>
{% endblock %}
